<p>Hungarians voted on Sunday in a parliamentary election that could end Prime Minister Viktor Orban’s 16-year hold on power, with polls showing a close race between Orban’s ruling Fidesz alliance and Peter Magyar’s insurgent Tisza party. Voters are choosing all 199 seats in the National Assembly in a single day, and results are expected after polls close at 7 p.m. local time. The race is drawing attention far beyond Hungary because the outcome could reshape the country’s place in Europe and echo in Moscow and Washington. It is also the strongest challenge Orban has faced in years, coming from a fast-rising rival whose campaign has tapped frustration over governance and the economy.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/qStt2rflzpC5hASBb7qO13qZBKN-NKXdxkOup3ZDD0Tiau6BQVA7YT9JyaQMcp_ljof9ytxlqDxM_ZKwECPkN8PqDA_L1LUPDN9fzLLyGRuWTVcc8NIDC9xYeD0Bn6whOT0FP4K0jiB22zrhsNGXgieGD9DLBhraicIRwhD-FF9Anu9zlkGGnChBjWng86-LLnPyDlYXDoYYKlttRFJBy3G6PRtBehGK' alt='Viktor Orban, Hungary’s prime minister of 16 years, campaigning in Szekesfehervar, Hungary, on Friday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Campaign disruption: Le Monde reported that Orban had to abandon his usual closed-door, invitation-only rallies and even faced heckling in Gyor late in the campaign, a sign of unusual pressure on a leader seeking a fifth straight term.</li><li>Grassroots challenge: The BBC described Magyar as the face of a grassroots party, underscoring how his campaign’s momentum comes from a newer movement rather than the entrenched machine Fidesz built over years in power.</li><li>Governance as campaign fuel: Magyar’s rise has drawn strength from anger over alleged corruption and sluggish economic growth, making competence in government one of the election’s central issues.</li><li>Trump factor: Orban’s alignment with President Trump has turned the race into a test case for the European hard right, but German media noted that Trump’s endorsement may not help much with voters wary of anti-Europe rhetoric.</li></ul><blockquote>This youth who care about politics, we&#x27;ve never seen anything like it - concert organizer quoted by Le Monde</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Viktor Orban and Fidesz: Orban has framed the election as a fight over sovereignty, arguing that Hungary should keep charting its own course rather than bend to pressure from Brussels on migration, sanctions, and Ukraine-related questions. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/news/638150-hungary-crucial-election-updates/'>RT</a>)</li><li>Peter Magyar and Tisza: Magyar has tried to channel anti-corruption anger into a promise to fix everyday state functions, presenting Tisza as a competence-and-reform alternative rather than simply an anti-Orban protest vehicle. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/12/world/europe/who-peter-magyar-hungary-election-opposition-orban.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Artists and youth turnout organizers: Musicians and concert organizers have cast their role less as party campaigning and more as motivating first-time and younger voters who might otherwise stay home. (<a href='https://www.npr.org/2026/04/11/nx-s1-5773325/at-a-concert-in-budapest-anti-orban-sentiments-take-center-stage-ahead-of-election'>NPR</a>, <a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/04/11/in-hungary-at-the-end-the-system-concert-this-youth-who-care-about-politics-we-ve-never-seen-anything-like-it_6752320_4.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>European Union observers: In Brussels, many see the vote as a test of whether one of the bloc’s most persistent internal dissenters could become more predictable—or keep frustrating partners—from inside the EU. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/hungarians-vote-in-landmark-election-closely-watched-by-eu-russia-us'>Reuters via The Straits Times</a>, <a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/apr/12/hungarians-vote-in-hard-fought-election-that-could-oust-viktor-orban-after-16-years'>The Guardian</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sourc